---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Workforce">
  <main>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="workforce-kicker">People are the capacity system</span>
    <h2>This cannot be staffed by moving boxes on an organization chart</h2>
    <p class="desc">Coverage, facilities, medicines, records, and specialist
      routing work only when qualified people are available in the right role,
      region, modality, and schedule. The plan therefore treats workforce
      as a hard constraint: headcount is discounted for leave, competency,
      schedule availability, retention, burnout, and unsafe overtime before it
      counts as effective capacity.</p>
    <div class="workforce-challenge-grid"
      aria-label="Healthcare workforce challenges the plan must solve">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <div><h3>National shortages are geographically unequal</h3>
          <p>Nonmetropolitan nursing gaps, specialist scarcity, and long travel
            distances can leave a nominally covered rural resident without
            usable care.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <div><h3>Rural readiness is larger than visit volume</h3>
          <p>A low-volume community still needs observation, emergency handoff,
            pharmacy, transport, maternal and pediatric triage, and weather
            resilience.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <div><h3>Training arrives years after funding</h3>
          <p>A funded slot becomes capacity only after faculty, clinical
            rotations, completion, credentialing, and a matched service
            location.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>04</span>
        <div><h3>Burnout destroys nominal capacity</h3>
          <p>Vacancies, turnover, leave, moral injury, and excessive overtime
            reduce safe capacity even when an authorized position exists on
            paper.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>05</span>
        <div><h3>New benefits release hidden demand</h3>
          <p>Long-term care, behavioral health, dental, vision, hearing, EMS,
            and public-health access cannot expand faster than trained teams
            and safe facilities.</p></div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>06</span>
        <div><h3>Administrative simplification displaces workers</h3>
          <p>Private claims, prior authorization, PBM rebate, brokerage, and
            revenue-cycle work shrinks before every worker has a new role.</p></div>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-target-strip" aria-label="Controlled workforce targets">
      <article><strong>&le;5%</strong><span>critical-role vacancy</span><small>controlled floor</small></article>
      <article><strong>&ge;55,000</strong><span>public training slots each year</span><small>controlled floor</small></article>
      <article><strong>&ge;96%</strong><span>service-obligation fulfillment</span><small>controlled floor</small></article>
      <article><strong>&ge;30%</strong><span>burnout-risk reduction</span><small>controlled floor</small></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="workforce-kicker">Labor transition ledger</span>
    <h2>Administrative work shrinks: what ends, what moves, and what remains</h2>
    <p class="desc">The assessment separates positions whose current function
      contracts, workers whose skills transfer to public operations, and
      clinical time released from paperwork. Those are different effects and
      cannot be added together. Counts use BLS employment anchors, explicit
      exposure shares, and overlap controls. Choose a case to test the
      transition load.</p>
    <div class="workforce-scenario-controls" role="group"
      aria-label="Workforce transition planning case">
      <button type="button" data-wf-scenario="low">Lower exposure</button>
      <button type="button" data-wf-scenario="plan" aria-pressed="true">Planning case</button>
      <button type="button" data-wf-scenario="high">Stress case</button>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-count-grid workforce-count-grid-five" aria-live="polite">
      <article>
        <span>Current functions that contract</span>
        <strong id="wf-eliminated">760,000</strong>
        <small>derived, Medium-Low confidence</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Gross funded positions created</span>
        <strong id="wf-created">510,000</strong>
        <small>derived floor, Medium-Low confidence</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Fillable by affected workers</span>
        <strong id="wf-inside">360,000</strong>
        <small>skills match, Medium confidence</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Other entrants required</span>
        <strong id="wf-new-hires">150,000</strong>
        <small>derived residual, Medium-Low confidence</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Placement or training target</span>
        <strong id="wf-supported">570,000</strong>
        <small>controlled 75% worker-protection floor</small>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-ledger-grid" aria-label="Three labor effects that must not be combined">
      <article>
        <span>1 &middot; Position contraction</span>
        <strong>Payroll functions become unnecessary</strong>
        <p>Duplicated enrollment, network, billing, denial, rebate, and
          employer-plan work can end. Attrition and phased conversion affect
          how many people actually need a new job at one time.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>2 &middot; Work transfer</span>
        <strong id="wf-ledger-transfer">360,000 skills matches</strong>
        <p>Eligibility, payment integrity, records, navigation, rights,
          procurement, cyber, and assurance still require people. Ownership
          and purpose change even when the underlying skill remains useful.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>3 &middot; Time released</span>
        <strong>About 282,000 FTE-equivalent</strong>
        <p>Applying the AMA's 13 weekly prior-authorization hours per physician
          to 866,460 direct-patient-care physicians shows the scale of practice
          capacity tied up. It is not a layoff count and overlaps some billing
          work, so it is never added to the position estimate. Confidence:
          Low for the FTE conversion.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-flow" aria-label="Planning flow from eliminated jobs to new positions">
      <div class="workforce-flow-column">
        <span class="workforce-flow-label">Legacy work removed</span>
        <strong id="wf-flow-eliminated">760k</strong>
        <div class="workforce-flow-track"><i id="wf-flow-eliminated-bar"></i></div>
        <small>insurance, billing, PBM, brokerage, and duplicative vendor work</small>
      </div>
      <div class="workforce-flow-bridge" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</div>
      <div class="workforce-flow-destinations">
        <article>
          <div><span>Inside the new system</span><strong id="wf-flow-inside">360k</strong></div>
          <div class="workforce-flow-track"><i id="wf-flow-inside-bar"></i></div>
        </article>
        <article>
          <div><span>External placement or approved training needed to reach the 75% floor</span>
            <strong id="wf-flow-external">210k</strong></div>
          <div class="workforce-flow-track secondary"><i id="wf-flow-external-bar"></i></div>
        </article>
        <article>
          <div><span>Attrition, retirement, other work, or unresolved support gap</span>
            <strong id="wf-flow-gap">190k</strong></div>
          <div class="workforce-flow-track muted"><i id="wf-flow-gap-bar"></i></div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-legacy-layout">
      <div>
        <span class="workforce-section-label">Select a legacy function</span>
        <div id="workforce-legacy-list" class="workforce-legacy-list"></div>
      </div>
      <div id="workforce-legacy-detail" class="workforce-legacy-detail"
        aria-live="polite"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-continuing">
      <span class="workforce-section-label">Functions that continue under public administration</span>
      <div class="workforce-continuing-grid">
        <article><strong>Eligibility and payment</strong><span>one enrollment record, provider payment, reconciliation, and continuity</span></article>
        <article><strong>Integrity and appeals</strong><span>fraud control, clinical review for exceptional cases, patient rights, and correction</span></article>
        <article><strong>Records and data quality</strong><span>clinical documentation, coding for care and quality, privacy, and portability</span></article>
        <article><strong>Medicines operations</strong><span>formulary evidence, pharmacy claims, procurement, inventory, quality, and shortage response</span></article>
        <article><strong>Technology and resilience</strong><span>cybersecurity, identity, conformance, audit evidence, downtime, and recovery</span></article>
        <article><strong>Worker transition</strong><span>income bridges, retraining, placement, hardship review, and legacy wind-down</span></article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">BLS anchors are not added together mechanically:
      611,100 jobs in direct health and medical insurance carriers,
      229,070 insurance claims and policy-processing clerks, and 417,500
      billing and posting clerks are overlapping or broader-than-health
      populations. The planning model counts functions once. The 194,570
      medical-records specialists are treated as continuing or redeployable
      work, not as an eliminated occupation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Where the 510,000 gross positions are created</h2>
    <p class="desc">The planning case funds positions in seven operating
      functions. The total is gross: it counts jobs the system must staff,
      whether the worker comes from a contracting administrative function,
      an existing care site, a training program, or another employer. Select
      a row to see the derivation and transition path.</p>
    <div id="workforce-created-chart" class="workforce-created-chart"
      aria-label="New system positions by function"></div>
    <div class="workforce-reconcile" aria-live="polite">
      <article><span>Gross system positions</span><strong id="wf-reconcile-created">510,000</strong></article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&minus;</i>
      <article><span>Fillable from affected pool</span><strong id="wf-reconcile-inside">360,000</strong></article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">=</i>
      <article><span>Other entrants needed</span><strong id="wf-reconcile-entrants">150,000</strong></article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-scope-warning">
      <strong>Do not call the subtraction a national net-jobs forecast.</strong>
      <p>Inside the quantified boundary, <span id="wf-scope-eliminated">760,000</span>
        current functions contract while <span id="wf-scope-created">510,000</span>
        gross positions are funded, a difference of <span id="wf-scope-difference">250,000</span>
        positions. The model does not yet count how many unit jobs are filled
        by workers from converted clinics, or the added labor required for
        long-term care, behavioral health, dental, vision, hearing, EMS, and
        public health. Calling that difference a national job loss would mix
        known and uncalibrated populations.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="note">This is deliberately a floor. It excludes the still-open
      national workforce quantity for long-term care, behavioral health,
      dental, vision, hearing, EMS, and public health. Those benefit-workforce
      formulas are not yet fixed and must be added before a total
      net-employment claim is valid.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card workforce-labor-supply">
    <span class="workforce-kicker">Labor-force sufficiency test</span>
    <h2>America can cover the quantified floor nationally, but not without solving clinical and rural shortages</h2>
    <p class="desc">The aggregate labor pool is large enough for the modeled
      hiring pace. The binding constraints are occupation, license, training
      time, faculty, location, and retention. Existing federal projections
      show that several of the hardest roles are already in shortage.</p>
    <div class="workforce-labor-grid" aria-live="polite">
      <article><span>Other entrants over rollout</span><strong id="wf-labor-entrants">150,000</strong><small>after internal role matching</small></article>
      <article><span>Average over 12 years</span><strong id="wf-labor-annual">12,500/year</strong><small>timing will be uneven by phase</small></article>
      <article><span>Share of 2024 employment</span><strong id="wf-labor-share">0.09%</strong><small>169.96 million jobs nationally</small></article>
      <article><span>Gross jobs filled by transition</span><strong id="wf-labor-transition">71%</strong><small>planning skills match</small></article>
      <article><span>Training target versus pace</span><strong id="wf-training-ratio">4.4&times;</strong><small>slots are not completed hires</small></article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-supply-layout">
      <div>
        <span class="workforce-section-label">Scale comparison</span>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="workforce-supply-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Annual scale</th><th>What it establishes</th><th>Confidence</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Modeled other-entrant need</td><td id="wf-table-annual">12,500</td><td>Average pace after affected-worker matches, not a yearly hiring schedule</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Publicly funded training target</td><td>&ge;55,000</td><td>Enough numerical capacity to cover the pace only if slots match roles and graduates complete</td><td>Controlled</td></tr>
              <tr><td>All healthcare occupation openings</td><td>1.9 million</td><td>A large active labor market, but most openings include replacement demand and are not spare workers</td><td>High</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Registered-nurse openings</td><td>189,100</td><td>Pipeline scale is far larger than this plan's average increment, but employers already compete for it</td><td>High</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="workforce-shortage-panel">
        <span class="workforce-section-label">Baseline shortage warning</span>
        <strong>Training must expand supply, not just reshuffle it</strong>
        <div class="workforce-shortage-grid">
          <article><b>108,960</b><span>projected RN shortage in 2038</span></article>
          <article><b>141,160</b><span>projected physician shortage in 2038</span></article>
          <article><b>11%</b><span>projected nonmetro RN shortage</span></article>
          <article><b>58%</b><span>projected nonmetro physician shortage</span></article>
        </div>
        <p>These HRSA projections assume current patterns. They show why a
          national headcount can look feasible while particular communities
          remain impossible to staff. Confidence: Medium for projected
          magnitude and geography.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-risk-matrix">
      <span class="workforce-section-label">Where the labor test passes and where it can fail</span>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Public administration, payment, data, and assurance</strong><b data-level="strong">Strong match</b><span>330,000 of 331,000 planning positions are mapped to affected administrative workers. Public hiring speed, training, pay, and location still matter.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Diagnostic-treatment unit teams</strong><b data-level="tight">Constrained</b><span>108,000 planning positions require clinicians, technicians, graduates, or transfers from existing care sites.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Rural flex and travel pool</strong><b data-level="high">High risk</b><span>All 30,000 planning positions require clinical or technical entrants, and rural vacancy projections are already worse than metropolitan projections.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Faculty, preceptors, and training support</strong><b data-level="high">Bottleneck</b><span>Eleven thousand positions are small in national terms but determine whether the 55,000 annual training slots become completed credentials.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Expanded benefits outside the floor</strong><b data-level="unknown">Not yet sized</b><span>Long-term care, behavioral health, dental, vision, hearing, EMS, and public-health staffing can materially raise demand after their operating standards are fixed.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-verdict">
      <strong>Assessment</strong>
      <p><b>Administrative labor: sufficient in arithmetic terms.</b> The
        affected workforce is larger than the public administrative roles it
        can plausibly fill. <b>Clinical labor: achievable only as a phased
        capacity program.</b> The average annual increment is modest nationally,
        but present shortages make training completion, compensation, scope,
        rural support, and retention mandatory operating conditions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card workforce-immigration">
    <span class="workforce-kicker">Merit immigration fills the shortage the pipeline cannot</span>
    <h2>A merit-based health-talent pathway closes the clinical and rural gap on time</h2>
    <p class="desc">Domestic training is the primary answer, but it arrives
      years after funding and cannot fill the hardest clinical and rural roles
      fast enough. A disciplined merit immigration pathway is the bridge. It
      recruits qualified clinicians for the exact roles and places that are
      short, holds them to the adopted pay bands, recognizes their credentials
      quickly, and shrinks as the domestic pipeline matures. The design copies
      what has actually worked in other countries rather than the visa lottery
      it replaces.</p>
    <div class="workforce-labor-grid">
      <article><span>Other-entrant gap to close</span><strong>150,000</strong><small>over the twelve-year rollout, after internal role matching</small></article>
      <article><span>Concentrated in hard roles</span><strong>138,000</strong><small>of the gap is unit-clinical and rural-clinical, not administrative</small></article>
      <article><span>Physicians already IMG-trained</span><strong>25.6%</strong><small>active U.S. physicians in 2024, a proven integration base</small></article>
      <article><span>Nurses internationally educated</span><strong>17.8%</strong><small>of the U.S. registered-nurse workforce</small></article>
      <article><span>Target decision speed</span><strong>&le;2 weeks</strong><small>service standard for shortage-role petitions, met 80% of the time</small></article>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-supply-layout">
      <div>
        <span class="workforce-section-label">Evidence: programs deemed successful and what they prove</span>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="workforce-supply-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Success tenet it demonstrates</th><th>Sourced signal</th><th>Confidence</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Canada Global Talent Stream and Express Entry health draws</td><td>Speed, predictability, and occupation targeting</td><td>Work permits in about two weeks and labour-market decisions in ten business days, each met 80% of the time; dedicated healthcare selection draws since 2023</td><td>High</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Australia SkillSelect points test</td><td>Transparent merit ranking from an applicant pool</td><td>Ranked selection from expressions of interest with a published 65-point entry floor and higher invited scores</td><td>High</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Germany EU Blue Card and Opportunity Card</td><td>Shortage-tuned thresholds plus a points job-search entry</td><td>A lower salary threshold for approved shortage occupations and a six-point job-search card introduced in 2024</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
              <tr><td>United States Conrad 30 and J-1 waiver</td><td>Place-based service in exchange for status</td><td>Waives the two-year return rule for physicians who serve at least three years in a designated HPSA, MUA, or MUP</td><td>High</td></tr>
              <tr><td>WHO Global Code and safeguards list</td><td>Ethical, managed recruitment that protects source countries</td><td>Voluntary 2010 standard adopted by member states and a 2023 list of countries that should not be actively recruited from</td><td>High</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="workforce-shortage-panel">
        <span class="workforce-section-label">The common tenets</span>
        <strong>Every durable program shares the same spine</strong>
        <div class="workforce-shortage-grid">
          <article><b>Merit</b><span>selection weighted to real shortage roles</span></article>
          <article><b>Speed</b><span>a fast, published processing standard</span></article>
          <article><b>Bridge</b><span>credential recognition and licensing</span></article>
          <article><b>Stay</b><span>a real path to permanent residency</span></article>
        </div>
        <p>The unsuccessful pattern is the opposite: random allocation, slow and
          opaque queues, no credential path, and a status that never settles.
          Confidence: High that these features distinguish the programs above.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-rural-steps" aria-label="Eight design tenets of the merit health-talent pathway">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Select on merit for the roles actually short</h3><p>Points and criteria weight qualification, competency, language safety, and willingness to serve where shortages are worst. NHTIB publishes the shortage-occupation and shortage-region lists that drive selection, not a lottery.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Make processing fast and predictable</h3><p>A published service standard targets a median decision of about two weeks for shortage-role petitions, met 80% of the time, so qualified clinicians do not choose a faster country first.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Protect domestic pay and workers</h3><p>Every admission is priced at or above the adopted NPCB role-region band. Employers carry a labor-market benefit obligation, and no admission may be used to undercut wages or displace a domestic worker or trainee.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Recognize credentials and bridge licensing</h3><p>HCRB runs national competency assessment, supervised-practice bridges, and licensing coordination with states so a qualified IMG or IEN reaches full license quickly instead of leaving the field.</p></article>
      <article><span>05</span><h3>Offer a clear path to permanent residency</h3><p>Retention comes from a durable status with family inclusion, not a revolving temporary permit. People who serve and integrate can stay.</p></article>
      <article><span>06</span><h3>Tie some entry to underserved service</h3><p>A rural and HPSA service pathway, modeled on Conrad 30, routes a defined share of arrivals into the hardest-to-staff places and hands placement to RS-CORPS.</p></article>
      <article><span>07</span><h3>Recruit ethically</h3><p>Selection complies with the WHO code and the safeguards list, uses bilateral training-and-return agreements, and does not drain the health systems of countries already in shortage.</p></article>
      <article><span>08</span><h3>Complement training, never replace it</h3><p>The pathway closes the near-term gap that domestic training cannot fill in time. Admission volumes are sized down automatically as NHWECA training completion rises.</p></article>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-risk-matrix">
      <span class="workforce-section-label">Where immigration helps most and where it cannot substitute</span>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Physicians and specialists</strong><b data-level="strong">High yield</b><span>A quarter of active U.S. physicians are already internationally trained, so targeted admission plus faster credentialing can move the 141,160 projected 2038 physician shortage materially.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Registered and advanced-practice nurses</strong><b data-level="tight">Moderate yield</b><span>Internationally educated nurses are an established pipeline, but English-for-safety, licensure by state, and ethical-sourcing limits cap the pace against the 108,960 projected 2038 RN shortage.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Rural and HPSA placement</strong><b data-level="high">Only with a service obligation</b><span>Immigration reaches the 58% projected nonmetro physician shortage only when entry is tied to a bounded rural or HPSA service term, because arrivals gravitate to metropolitan areas otherwise.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Faculty and preceptors</strong><b data-level="tight">Force multiplier</b><span>Admitting experienced educators relieves the training bottleneck that decides whether 55,000 annual slots become completed credentials.</span></div>
      <div class="workforce-risk-row"><strong>Total domestic capacity</strong><b data-level="unknown">Not a substitute</b><span>Immigration is a bridge, not the plan. It cannot excuse under-training, pay suppression, or unsafe staffing, and it shrinks as the domestic pipeline matures.</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-agencies">
      <span class="workforce-section-label">Accountable immigration institutions</span>
      <div class="workforce-agency-grid">
        <article><strong>NHTIB</strong><span>merit selection, shortage-occupation and shortage-region lists, service standards, and admission caps tied to role-region need</span></article>
        <article><strong>HCRB</strong><span>credential recognition, competency assessment, supervised-practice bridges, and state licensing coordination</span></article>
        <article><strong>RS-CORPS</strong><span>placement, housing, and retention support for service-obligated arrivals in rural and HPSA sites</span></article>
        <article><strong>NPCB</strong><span>the published pay floors every admission must meet, so immigration cannot lower the price of labor</span></article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-pay-guardrail">
      <strong>Immigration adds capacity; it does not lower the price of labor.</strong>
      <p>A merit pathway is valid only when admissions meet the adopted pay band,
        do not displace a domestic worker or trainee, comply with the WHO
        safeguards list, and shrink as domestic completion rises. It is enacted
        by Title VII of the enabling act together with controlled conforming
        amendments to the Immigration and Nationality Act, including a
        shortage-occupation designation and a health-talent selection category.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/employment-social-development/services/foreign-workers/global-talent.html"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">Canada Global Talent Stream service standards</a>;
      <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/working-in-the-united-states/students-and-exchange-visitors/conrad-30-waiver-program"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">Conrad 30 J-1 waiver program</a>;
      <a href="https://www.who.int/teams/health-workforce/migration/practice/who-global-code-of-practice-on-the-international-recruitment-of-health-personnel"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO Global Code and safeguards list</a>;
      <a href="https://www.aamc.org/data-reports/data/2025-key-findings"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">AAMC physician-workforce data</a>;
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/workforce_transition_methodology.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">workforce sources and methodology</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card workforce-compensation">
    <span class="workforce-kicker">Compensation is a capacity control</span>
    <h2>What clinicians would be paid, and what has not been set</h2>
    <p class="desc">The system cannot justify savings by cutting clinical pay.
      It must budget the full cost of attracting and retaining enough qualified
      people to meet safe staffing, access, and readiness obligations. Exact
      national salary bands have not been calibrated. The operating rules
      below define how those bands must be built and when they must change.</p>

    <div class="workforce-pay-equation"
      aria-label="Clinician compensation band formula">
      <article>
        <span>Audited base band</span>
        <strong>role + specialty + region + setting</strong>
        <p>Start with observed compensation and the real labor market for the
          work being staffed.</p>
      </article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">+</i>
      <article>
        <span>Authorized adjustments</span>
        <strong>scarcity + rural + call + complexity</strong>
        <p>Add visible premiums for hard-to-fill work, readiness, leadership,
          supervision, and training.</p>
      </article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">=</i>
      <article>
        <span>Published total compensation</span>
        <strong>pay that can actually staff safe FTE</strong>
        <p>Test the result against vacancy, retention, workload, burnout, and
          regional access evidence.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-pay-rules">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>Pay for all required work</h3>
        <p>Compensate visits, e-consults, protocol work, call, readiness,
          supervision, training, required follow-up, and explanation time.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>Use pay to correct scarcity</h3>
        <p>Raise an authorized role-region band when vacancies, queues,
          turnover, or geographic gaps show that the current offer cannot
          secure safe capacity.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>Do not pay for harmful volume</h3>
        <p>Compensation cannot reward unsafe throughput, denials,
          under-referral, suppressed demand, or geographic abandonment.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>04</span>
        <h3>Keep the labor cost visible</h3>
        <p>Publish total compensation and its source. Allocate clinician pay
          once so it is not hidden inside both a hospital budget and a
          separate professional payment.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-pay-benchmarks">
      <div class="workforce-pay-benchmarks-head">
        <div>
          <span class="workforce-section-label">Current national anchors</span>
          <h3>Observed pay sets the calibration scale</h3>
        </div>
        <p>These are 2024 or 2025 national observations, not proposed salaries.
          Final bands can differ by region, specialty, setting, schedule,
          scarcity, and responsibility.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tbl-scroll">
        <table class="data workforce-pay-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Role</th><th>Observed annual pay</th><th>How it is used</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>Physicians</th>
              <td><strong>~$330k</strong> primary care median<br>
                <strong>~$417k</strong> specialist average
                <span class="conf medium">medium</span></td>
              <td>Starting evidence for specialty bands, not a national cap.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Registered nurses</th>
              <td><strong>$93,600</strong> median
                <span class="conf high">high</span></td>
              <td>Baseline before scarcity, rural, shift, call, or leadership adjustments.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Advanced practice nurses</th>
              <td><strong>$132,050</strong> combined median
                <span class="conf high">high</span></td>
              <td>Role-specific bands still distinguish nurse practitioners,
                nurse midwives, and nurse anesthetists.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Physician assistants</th>
              <td><strong>$133,260</strong> median
                <span class="conf high">high</span></td>
              <td>Adjusted for function, supervision, setting, call, and region.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Pharmacists</th>
              <td><strong>$137,480</strong> median
                <span class="conf high">high</span></td>
              <td>Clinical, hospital, community, and public-production duties remain distinct.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Physical and occupational therapists</th>
              <td><strong>$101,020</strong> PT; <strong>$98,340</strong> OT medians
                <span class="conf high">high</span></td>
              <td>Home, facility, rehabilitation, and regional scarcity affect the band.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Respiratory therapists</th>
              <td><strong>$80,450</strong> median
                <span class="conf high">high</span></td>
              <td>Readiness, night coverage, critical care, and rural scarcity can add premiums.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Paramedics</th>
              <td><strong>$58,410</strong> median
                <span class="conf high">high</span></td>
              <td>Schedule, readiness, transport geography, and extended shifts must be priced.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="workforce-pay-guardrail">
      <strong>A cheaper system is valid only at full safe-workforce cost.</strong>
      <p>A cost result fails if it requires pay below the adopted band,
        uncompensated labor, unfilled positions, unsafe overtime, untrained
        scope expansion, or a worsening safety culture. Compensation is
        increased, not suppressed, when pay is the evidenced cause of a
        critical vacancy or retention failure.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">BLS healthcare occupation pay data</a>;
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/workforce_transition_methodology.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">compensation sources and methodology</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>A rural staffing plan built around a permanent core</h2>
    <p class="desc">Travel staff are a relief valve, not the rural model.
      Every region first funds a permanent local core and the facilities,
      housing, supervision, transport, and family support that make retention
      possible. A public regional flex pool then covers vacancies, leave,
      training, seasonal demand, and short surges without forcing unsafe
      overtime.</p>
    <div class="workforce-rural-steps" aria-label="Seven-step rural staffing plan">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Measure safe FTE need</h3><p>Count readiness, leave, supervision, geography, weather, EMS handoff, and low-volume essential service.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Anchor Type C hubs</h3><p>Pair enhanced rural units with Type A/B satellites, mobile outreach, pharmacy, hospital, and transport capacity.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Fund the permanent core</h3><p>Use transparent rural premiums, call pay, housing, childcare, spouse support, schedule control, and local leadership.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Build the local pipeline</h3><p>Reserve training slots, rural rotations, scholarships, community recruitment, and bounded service obligations with hardship rights.</p></article>
      <article><span>05</span><h3>Deploy the flex pool</h3><p>RS-CORPS supplies rotating nurses, paramedics, imaging/lab staff, respiratory therapy, pharmacy, and behavioral health.</p></article>
      <article><span>06</span><h3>Bring specialists to the team</h3><p>Use e-consults, tele-specialty, tele-radiology, tele-ICU, and cross-region queue capacity without double-counting supply.</p></article>
      <article><span>07</span><h3>Gate expansion on retention</h3><p>Hold or resize benefit waves when vacancy, burnout, EMS, home-care, behavioral-health, or hospital staffing floors fail.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-rotation">
      <div>
        <span class="workforce-section-label">Illustrative five-team regional nurse pod</span>
        <h3>Four teams deployed; one team resetting, training, and handing off</h3>
        <p>Each 10-week cycle contains an eight-week deployment and a protected
          two-week reset. Staggering the reset keeps relief capacity continuous.
          Assignments rotate among communities so the pool fills gaps without
          replacing local jobs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tbl-scroll">
        <table class="data workforce-rotation-table">
          <caption class="sr-only">Staggered ten-week travel-nurse rotation</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Team</th><th>Wk 1-2</th><th>Wk 3-4</th><th>Wk 5-6</th><th>Wk 7-8</th><th>Wk 9-10</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>A</th><td class="reset">Reset</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td></tr>
            <tr><th>B</th><td>Deploy</td><td class="reset">Reset</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td></tr>
            <tr><th>C</th><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td class="reset">Reset</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td></tr>
            <tr><th>D</th><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td class="reset">Reset</td><td>Deploy</td></tr>
            <tr><th>E</th><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td>Deploy</td><td class="reset">Reset</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-rural-formula">
      <span>Flex-pool sizing rule</span>
      <strong>max(vacancy gap + protected leave + training backfill + seasonal surge, continuity floor)</strong>
      <p>The planning case holds 30,000 rural flex positions nationally.
        Its provisional mix assigns 18,000 to rotating RN/LPN nursing and
        12,000 to EMS, respiratory, imaging/lab, pharmacy, behavioral-health,
        and supervisory relief. Those numbers are derived here, not fixed
        targets, and would be replaced by the role-region staffing formula
        once it is adopted.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>AI and telehealth add capacity; they do not erase bedside work</h2>
    <p class="desc">Technology removes avoidable clerical load, carries
      expertise across distance, and helps a small local team close the loop.
      It does not count as capacity twice, authorize unsafe staffing, or make
      final clinical decisions without an accountable professional.</p>
    <div class="workforce-tech-grid">
      <article>
        <span>AI-assisted workflow</span>
        <h3>Give time back to clinicians</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Draft encounter documentation and patient instructions for human review.</li>
          <li>Check referral packets, result closure, medication lists, and follow-up queues.</li>
          <li>Forecast staffing, transport, inventory, and surge demand by region.</li>
          <li>Support triage and translation while preserving override, audit, and manual fallback.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Telehealth and e-consults</span>
        <h3>Move expertise before moving the patient</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Local teams acquire the history, exam, labs, imaging, and vital signs.</li>
          <li>Specialists answer complete e-consults within a 24-hour median target.</li>
          <li>Tele-specialty, tele-radiology, tele-ICU, and behavioral health cover distance.</li>
          <li>Hands-on procedures, unstable patients, and red flags still trigger transfer.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-capacity-path"
      aria-label="Capacity path from local care through telehealth to transfer">
      <article><span>1</span><h3>Local team</h3><p>Exam, tests, stabilization</p></article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
      <article><span>2</span><h3>AI support</h3><p>Document, check, surface risk</p></article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
      <article><span>3</span><h3>E-consult</h3><p>Specialist response &le;24h</p></article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
      <article><span>4</span><h3>Tele-visit</h3><p>Cross-region expertise</p></article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
      <article><span>5</span><h3>Transfer when needed</h3><p>Procedure or higher acuity</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-tech-guardrail">
      <strong>Productivity is evidence-gated.</strong>
      <p>The clinical-productivity gain is deliberately left as a range
        (a 1.25x and a 2.25x branch), and neither is a staffing-cut
        instruction. Gains count only after safety, quality, workload,
        equity, and human-accountability tests pass.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The workforce transition across twelve years</h2>
    <div class="workforce-phase-strip" aria-label="Workforce rollout milestones">
      <article><span>P1-P2</span><strong>Years 2-3</strong><h3>Count need and protect workers</h3><p>Open registries, AHWCS, NHWECA, HATC, and RS-CORPS before legacy wind-down.</p></article>
      <article><span>P4</span><strong>Year 6</strong><h3>Pilot role and region models</h3><p>Test unit teams, compensation, scope, training, tele-support, and safety in representative regions.</p></article>
      <article><span>P5</span><strong>Year 7</strong><h3>Scale workforce reforms</h3><p>Expand unit staffing, rural rotations, training slots, specialist routing, and scope floors.</p></article>
      <article><span>P7</span><strong>Year 10</strong><h3>Gate expanded benefits</h3><p>Do not expand faster than LTC, home-care, behavioral-health, DVH, EMS, and hospital capacity.</p></article>
      <article><span>P8</span><strong>Year 12</strong><h3>Prove mature performance</h3><p>Verify vacancies, placement, training, service obligations, burnout, safety culture, and bottleneck reduction.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="workforce-agencies">
      <span class="workforce-section-label">Accountable workforce institutions</span>
      <div class="workforce-agency-grid">
        <article><strong>AHWCS</strong><span>integrated workforce, compensation, scope, and education policy</span></article>
        <article><strong>NHWB</strong><span>role-region need, supply, vacancy, retention, and bottleneck plans</span></article>
        <article><strong>NPCB</strong><span>transparent compensation bands and rural/scarcity premiums</span></article>
        <article><strong>NCSWB</strong><span>national scope floors, competency, supervision, and escalation</span></article>
        <article><strong>NHWECA</strong><span>training slots, scholarships, fellowships, hubs, and faculty support</span></article>
        <article><strong>RS-CORPS</strong><span>rural placement, housing, rotations, transport, and retention</span></article>
        <article><strong>HATC</strong><span>income bridge, retraining, placement, retention, and appeal</span></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>What is controlled, derived, and still open</h2>
    <div class="workforce-limit-grid">
      <article><span>Controlled</span><h3>Performance floors</h3><p>Placement, staffing, vacancy, bottleneck, training, service, scope, burnout, and safety targets are set as specified floors.</p></article>
      <article><span>Derived</span><h3>Job quantities and the travel pool</h3><p>The 760,000 eliminated-position case, 360,000 internal matches, 510,000 new-position floor, and 30,000 flex pool are transparent planning estimates.</p></article>
      <article><span>Open</span><h3>The audited national workforce total and pay bands</h3><p>Role-region FTE formulas, compensation bands and adjustment amounts, employment boundaries, benefit standards, and phase resource allocation are still to be fixed.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/workforce_transition_methodology.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources for compensation, staffing, and job-count methodology</a>.</p>
  </section>

